  (:adr/title "ADR-2607300200: the itonami fleet became callable — 67 actors, one entrance")
  (:adr/body "# ADR-2607300200: the itonami fleet became callable — 67 actors, one entrance

**Status**: accepted (all work on default branches; 67 actors live and verified)
**Date**: 2026-07-30
**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki (owner directives), agent execution

## Context

cloud-itonami holds ~1,348 repositories and one application. The fleet catalog
(ADR-2607300100) made the actors *visible*; nothing made them *callable*. Nine
actors had an address, 1,197 had no HTTP surface at all, and the one dispatch
namespace on the account — `ai-gftd-repository-dispatch`, created 2026-04-20 —
had been empty ever since.

## What is live

**One entrance.** `itonami-fleet-dispatch` (in cloud-itonami-app `services/`)
binds the dispatch namespace and routes `/{repo}/{path}` to the user Worker of
that name. Without it a namespaced Worker has no URL at all, which is why the
namespace sat unused for three months.

**67 actors**, each verified through that router returning `/health` 200
`ok:true`, and each carrying its address in its own blueprint so the catalog
and the deployment agree:

  cloud-itonami-isic-0111, and 66 iso3166 market-entry actors
  (ago…gnb, alphabetically contiguous)

**One fleet identity.** `itonami-fleet-kotobase-seed` in kagi (compartment
personal), did:key:z6MkqTPSr5ZUnLdroq3wMWVk9dxmNEKCLxbhse9Ec3Te6y28. One key
for the whole fleet was a decision, not a shortcut: `:apex` requires the graph
scope to equal the issuer DID, so the number of keys IS the number of refs a
Datalog join can reach. A key per actor would mean 1,197 refs and no
cross-sector query ever. The cost is stated rather than discovered — one key
compromised is the whole fleet, and per-actor attribution rests on
`marketplace.persist/stream-ctx`.

## Decisions worth carrying

**Nothing shared was built; three shared things already existed.** Each time
this session set out to extract a library, measurement found it already there:
`kotoba-lang/kotobase-client` for CACAO and storage, `kotoba-lang/marketplace`
for the actor host (449 lines, its docstring saying \"for any actor\"), and
`marketplace.persist` for the document/stream contexts. The itonami actors are
the first non-marketplace consumers of that host. The one repository that took
the other road, cloud-itonami-isic-6492, carries 1,229 lines of its own edge of
which 1,003 are generic.

**Per-actor cost is ~160 lines, and generation covers most of it.** For the
iso3166 family it is zero: a generator emits store adapter, worker and five
config files. That was only defensible after measuring that all 188 actors with
a bare three-letter suffix share a byte-identical `operation.cljc`, the same
13-method `Store` protocol, and exactly ONE MemStore code shape once strings
and comments are stripped.

**No bundle reuse, deliberately.** `governor.cljc` differs per country — 184
distinct code shapes even with strings stripped — and it compiles into the
bundle. Building once and patching the service name would ship one country's
compliance rules to another. Every actor is compiled from its own source.

**A shared compile cache was verified before being trusted.** It took builds
from ~180s to 69s, but a cache keyed on namespace names could have carried one
country's `marketentry.governor` into another's bundle — the same failure the
no-reuse rule prevents. Two attempts to check it were inconclusive (keywords
and docstrings do not survive `:advanced`, so their absence proves nothing).
The decisive test was comparing bundles: four builds, four sizes, four digests,
two of them differing in 159,127 bytes.

**No approval route on any actor.** They compile with the default in-memory
checkpointer, which does not survive a Worker isolate. A resume route would
look like one and reliably find nothing to resume, and an escalation that
silently never completes is how a governor gets bypassed. A durable
checkpointer is what makes that route honest.

## Corrections made during the work

Recorded because each was a confident claim that measurement overturned.

- **\"1,197 deployments is impossible.\"** Wrong, and the owner said so. I based
  it on the Cloudflare Pages project cap without checking whether the account
  had Workers for Platforms. It does, and the namespace was already there.
- **\"Three actors are not deployed.\"** They were — as Pages projects. I checked
  with `wrangler deployments list`, which only knows Workers.
- **\"Three duplicate actor ids.\"** None was a duplicate. One was a stale local
  directory under a pre-rename name; two were never pushed to GitHub at all.
  The generator was enumerating the filesystem while the rest of the same
  catalog already keyed on west.
- **\"One adapter serves 186 actors.\"** No: 184 distinct governors. The family
  shares transport, not logic.
- **\"iso3166 is 186 actors.\"** It is 223. Thirty-five are a country code plus
  an agency or subject (`jpn-meti`, `ind-clean-air`) with their own namespaces,
  and the generator does not apply to them. This surfaced only because a naive
  suffix extraction produced `air` as a country code and failed loudly; it
  could as easily have selected the wrong repositories in silence.
- **Deployed faster than the records were updated.** Thirteen actors were live
  and invisible to `fleet_search callable=true` because their blueprints had no
  endpoint — reintroducing exactly the drift this cleanup exists to remove.
  Fixed, and every deploy since records the address in the same step.

## Verification

- 67 / 67 actors answer `/health` 200 `ok:true` through the dispatcher.
- 67 / 67 blueprints carry their `:endpoint`, checked against GitHub rather
  than the local checkout.
- The dispatcher distinguishes its failure modes: unknown actor 404, invalid
  name 400, reachable-but-failing 502. The 404 took two attempts — `get()` is
  lazy, so a missing script surfaces at `fetch()`, and the first version
  reported every undeployed actor as a 502.
- cloud-itonami-app: 158 tests / 821 assertions, 0 failures; clj-kondo clean on
  the fleet namespaces.

## Not done

- **1,131 actors have no HTTP surface.** 122 remain in the iso3166 marketentry
  family, where the generator applies directly. The other ~1,009 span 557
  distinct `Store` protocols, 487 of them used by exactly one actor — that is
  1,009 individual implementations, not one repeated task.
- **The 35 non-marketentry iso3166 actors** need individual work.
- **No durable checkpointer**, so no approval routes.
- **`itonami-dispatch-probe`** — a 0.4 KiB test Worker in the namespace that
  cannot be removed with the tooling available: wrangler has no delete
  subcommand for a namespaced script, and the API call needs a bearer token
  this session does not hold (wrangler authenticates by OAuth).
- **No hostname bound** to the dispatcher. Deliberate while the fleet is small;
  binding one is a separate decision from proving the path.

## Resume point

Everything is on default branches; nothing is parked. To continue the
marketentry family:

```bash
# 1. select only bare three-letter suffixes — 35 iso3166 repos are not this family
ls -d orgs/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-iso3166-* \\
  | awk -F'iso3166-' '{print $2}' | awk 'length($0)==3'

# 2. worktrees in a west-sibling layout (the deps are :local/root)
# 3. python3 scripts/emit_iso3166_edge.py <ago worker.cljs> <worktrees…>
# 4. share node_modules and :cache-root across the batch
# 5. ship.sh: build -> deploy --secrets-file -> verify, one actor at a time
# 6. record the address in each blueprint — the step most easily forgotten
```

The generator and pipeline live in this session's scratchpad and are not
committed; they are ~200 lines and are reproducible from the description above,
but committing them would make the next batch cheaper.
")))
